      "2301.12110 Lemma 4.4 lem-ribbonmaya (ffschur.tex 1528)",
      "2301.12110 bead/hole conventions eq 4.1 (ffschur.tex 1449)"
    ],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "dictionary",
        "approach": "Prop 2.4 CLOSED IN FULL: cylindric shapes <-> C^{n,m} via x_i = R_i + i, where R_i is the right-hand column of row i (rows indexed DOWNWARD, so row lengths INCREASE and the sign is +i, not -i). Containment, box count per fundamental domain, and 'at most one box per column' <-> interlacing are all proved directly from that formula; no window/local-reduction argument is used. TWO errors made and corrected in session: (a) first draft claimed a bijection with periodic bead SETS -- false, a bead set determines the path only up to diagonal translation and the lost datum is Postnikov's shift d; (b) first draft of the sign used R_i - i -- false, it is not even strictly increasing. The sign was fixed by RENDERING WZZ Figure 5.1 and reading the boxes off the picture, not by guessing.",
        "trust": "proved",
        "file": "proofs/2026-09-20-c1-cylindric-M-convexity.tex",
        "sources": [
          "2301.12110 eq (4.1) (ffschur.tex 1449)"
        ],
        "role": "premise",
        "children": []
      },
      {
        "id": "decoupling",
        "approach": "Lemma 3.1: with x^{t-1}=p and x^{t+1}=r held fixed, the admissible middle rows x^t form a BOX prod_i [L_i,U_i], L_i = max(p_i, r_{i-1}+1), U_i = min(p_{i+1}-1, r_i). The two horizontal-strip conditions constrain x^t_i only through p and r, never through x^t_j for j != i; strict increase of x^t is automatic. Verified exhaustively: 380/380 feasible (p,r) instances over 2<=n<=5, 290 middle shapes total.",
        "trust": "proved",
        "file": "proofs/2026-09-20-c1-cylindric-M-convexity.tex",
        "role": "premise",
        "children": []
      },
      {
        "id": "palindrome-identity",
        "approach": "Lemma 3.2: sum_{i=1..m}(L_i+U_i) = sum_{i=1..m}(p_i+r_i). Proof is max+min=sum plus one wrap-around term, which contributes exactly +n. THE CYLINDRIC CASE WORKS BECAUSE OF THE PERIODICITY, NOT IN SPITE OF IT: drop the wrap-around and one gets sum(p+r)-n and the false conclusion that cylindric Bender-Knuth fails. I made exactly that slip in first derivation; the computation caught it. Verified 470/470 feasible (p,r) pairs over 2<=n<=6.",
        "trust": "proved",
        "file": "proofs/2026-09-20-c1-cylindric-M-convexity.tex",
        "role": "premise",
        "children": []
      },
      {
        "id": "cylindric-bender-knuth",
        "approach": "Cor 3.3: x^t_i -> L_i + U_i - x^t_i is an involution on chains interchanging alpha_t and alpha_{t+1}. Reproves Postnikov's symmetry theorem for cylindric skew Schur functions, so the argument does not have to cite it. Verified on COEFFICIENTS (not merely support) in 339 instances.",
        "trust": "proved",
        "file": "proofs/2026-09-20-c1-cylindric-M-convexity.tex",
        "role": "premise",
        "children": []
      },
      {
        "id": "exchange-move",
        "approach": "Cor 3.4: if alpha_t > alpha_{t+1} then alpha - e_t + e_{t+1} is a weight, realised by sliding ONE bead of row x^t one site left. This is the two-site Maya move the brief asked for. Proof: if x^t were at the left corner of its box then alpha_t = mu_- <= N/2 = alpha_{t+1} by the palindrome identity.",
        "trust": "proved",
