      {
        "id": "ribbon-maya-move-not-the-right-move",
        "approach": "The BROWSE addendum to the brief proposed Q198 (the converse of Naprienko 2301.12110 Lemma 4.4) as the opening move, on the reading that a ribbon's one-bead hop k -> K+1 is the shape of an M-convexity exchange. It is not the move this problem needs. A RIBBON of size r is one bead hopping r sites, jumping freely over occupied sites; a HORIZONTAL STRIP of size r is several beads hopping right with pairwise DISJOINT closed trajectories (Lemma 2.6). Cylindric skew Schur functions are built from horizontal strips, so the ribbon move is the wrong local move. The move that actually implements the exchange axiom is smaller: one bead in one row of the chain slides ONE site.",
        "trust": "dead-end",
        "reason": "Category error in the brief, not in Naprienko: Lemma 4.4 is about ribbons (rim hooks), and cylindric skew Schur tableaux are chains of horizontal strips. Ribbon bead-hops and horizontal-strip bead-hops are different moves -- the first jumps over beads, the second may not. Q198 remains a well-posed question about ribbons; it is simply not on the path to Q191.",
